########################## Validation on client input data acc to Schema validation/business logic #####################################

Request
   ↓
Schema validation
   ↓
Business validation
   ↓
Backend logic
   ↓
Database

Example: Our application only allows users who are at least 18 years old. (Business Logic)

age: int = PydanticField(
    ge=18,
    le=120
)
"Age must be an integer between 0 and 120" -- is a data/schema constraint.
"Only adults can register" - app/business rule/logic


Schema validation ------ handeled by pydantic/fastapi

Is the request structurally valid? 

example : 

age is an integer        ✅
age is -5                ❌
name is too long         ❌
name is missing          ❌


Business logic ------- handled by app/backend code

Does this request comply with our application's rules?

examples :

User must be 18+
Email must be unique
Order cannot exceed stock
User cannot cancel shipped order
Discount cannot exceed 30%

################################################## PUT VS PATCH ###########################################

PUT - replace the resource representation
PATCH - Modify only specific feilds

############################################## HTTP Headers ###############################################

Headers are metadata about an HTTP request or response. HTTP HAS 4 pieces

HTTP REQUEST
│
├── Method
│     POST
│
├── URL
│     /users
│
├── Headers
│     Content-Type: application/json
│     Authorization: Bearer ...
│
└── Body
      {
          "name": "Rahul",
          "age": 25
      }

This is the most important header for our current application.

----------->When we send:

Content-Type: application/json

we're telling FastAPI:

"The request body is JSON."

So:

Browser
   │
   │ Content-Type: application/json
   │
   │ {
   │   "name": "Rahul",
   │   "age": 25
   │ }
   ↓
FastAPI

FastAPI knows to parse the body as JSON.

####################### Authorization ##################################

This one:

Authorization: Bearer <token>

You've probably seen it in APIs.

For example:

GET /users/10
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOi...

Conceptually:

Client
   │
   │ Authorization: Bearer TOKEN
   ↓
Backend
   │
   │ verify token
   ↓
Allowed?
   │
   ├── No → 401
   │
   └── Yes
        ↓
      Process request

Status codes fit into this

Suppose the API requires authentication.

No credentials:

GET /users

could produce:

401 Unauthorized

Valid credentials but insufficient permissions:

DELETE /users/5

could produce:

403 Forbidden

So:

401
↓
"I don't have valid authentication."

403
↓
"I know who you are, but you're not allowed to do this."

This distinction is important.

############################## Login + Authentication ######################################
flow : 

             CLIENT
                │
                │ username + password
                ↓
             POST /login
                │
                ↓
             FASTAPI
                │
                │ verify credentials
                ↓
             TOKEN
                │
                ↓
             CLIENT
                │
                │ Authorization: Bearer TOKEN
                ↓
          Protected API

Our token is:

abc123

Every user gets the same token.

So:

Shah → abc123
Amit → abc123
Priya → abc123

That's obviously useless.

Also, the backend doesn't know:

Who owns this token?
When was it issued?
When does it expire?
What permissions does it represent?

A real authentication system needs answers to those questions.

That's where JWT comes in.

13. JWT will solve a different problem

A JWT can carry claims such as:

{
    "sub": "123",
    "role": "admin",
    "exp": 1780000000
}

and is cryptographically signed.

The flow becomes:

Login
  ↓
Verify username/password
  ↓
Create signed JWT
  ↓
Client
  ↓
Authorization: Bearer <JWT>
  ↓
Backend
  ↓
Verify JWT signature + claims
  ↓
Identify user
  ↓
Authorization/business logic

JWT - JAVA WEB TOKEN

. What JWT actually looks like

A JWT has three parts:

HEADER.PAYLOAD.SIGNATURE

Example:

eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.
eyJzdWIiOiJzaGFoIiwiZXhwIjoxNzU2ODAwMDAwfQ.
abc123xyz...

The three sections are:

Part	Purpose
Header	Algorithm and token type
Payload	Claims/data such as username, role, expiration
Signature	Proves the token was created by your backend and wasn't modified

################################################################################
JWT verification and extracting the AUTHENtICATED USER IN resuable dependency

Right now, your /protected endpoint verifies the JWT itself. That works, but if you have 20 protected endpoints, 
you don't want to repeat the same JWT verification code 20 times.

/login
   ↓
Create JWT
   ↓
Client receives JWT
   ↓
Authorization: Bearer <JWT>
   ↓
get_current_user()
   ↓
Verify JWT
   ↓
Extract username
   ↓
Endpoint

---username: str = Depends(get_current_user)

means:

Before executing protected_route(), FastAPI must execute get_current_user().

So the execution order is:

GET /protected
      ↓
HTTPBearer
      ↓
Extract JWT
      ↓
get_current_user()
      ↓
jwt.decode()
      ↓
Verify JWT
      ↓
Extract "sub"
      ↓
return "shah"
      ↓
protected_route(username="shah")

That's the important FastAPI concept here:

Depends() lets you create reusable logic that FastAPI executes before an endpoint.

Without a dependency:

@app.get("/users")
# verify JWT

@app.get("/orders")
# verify JWT

@app.get("/payments")
# verify JWT

@app.get("/profile")
# verify JWT

You would duplicate authentication code everywhere.

With the dependency:

@app.get("/users")
def users(username: str = Depends(get_current_user)):
    ...
@app.get("/orders")
def orders(username: str = Depends(get_current_user)):
    ...
@app.get("/profile")
def profile(username: str = Depends(get_current_user)):
    ...

The authentication logic exists in one place.

